msv1_0: Room-local Windows authentication between the processes of a prefix.
Programs that protect their own IPC with Windows authentication (NegotiateStream,
WCF named pipes, local HTTP with Negotiate) failed without a domain, because Samba's
ntlm_auth cannot accept logons on a Mac. When VIRTUAL_LOCAL_AUTH_HELPER and
VIRTUAL_LOCAL_AUTH_FILE are set (runtime/bin/virtual does this for every room),
msv1_0 uses Virtual's helper (winrt/sspi/ntlm_auth.py) for local targets
(host/localhost, HTTP/127.0.0.1:port, [::1], the computer name, or no target without
explicit credentials) and as acceptor. The helper creates a random identity per room
on first use (<prefix>/.virtual/local-identity.json, mode 0600) and verifies real
NTLMv2 proofs; only that room principal is mapped to the user's token. Remote targets
keep using Samba's ntlm_auth as before. Also: NTLM2 signing/sealing keys on the
acceptor side, SECPKG_ATTR_NAMES and SpGetContextToken for local logons, context
attributes report integrity/confidentiality, session keys are not traced for local logons.
Test (.NET NegotiateStream over a named pipe, EncryptAndSign): correct identity passes,
wrong password is rejected with SEC_E_LOGON_DENIED.

